-- The structural-recursion seed: the construct Catala must reject and the
-- relational middle-end admits.
--
-- `CONSIDER l WHEN EMPTY … WHEN x FOLLOWED BY xs …` is M1's ONE recursion
-- warrant (brief IR constraint 7; BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC R9). It lowers to a
-- two-clause definition whose head carries `[]` in one clause and `[X|Xs]` in
-- the other, so the decrease is visible in the emitted program rather than
-- asserted by a marker beside it — and `rpRecursion` records the argument
-- position, which the golden prints as `recursion: structural on argument 0`.
--
-- The decrease check is positional and syntactic: the recursive call must pass
-- the TAIL binder in the scrutinised slot. `not-ok/nonstructural.l4` is the
-- other side of that line (counting a NUMBER down has no decreasing list
-- argument at all) and is a batch LowerError.
--
-- `first or zero` is here to pin the case that is easy to get wrong in the
-- other direction: destructuring a list is not recursion, so it takes the same
-- two-clause shape and carries NO `recursion:` line.
--
-- Read the goldens together with `scores.l4`: the synthesised `member`
-- predicate there is the same two-clause list shape, minted by the middle-end
-- rather than written by an author.
--
-- Each directive below carries its L4-oracle value on the line after it, as a
-- `-- L4 oracle ==>` comment produced by `l4 run sumlist.l4`. It is the
-- differential harness's expectation, and it lives outside the emitted program
-- on purpose (BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC R11) — one copy per directive, so there is
-- nothing here for a later edit to leave behind.

@export
GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`running total` xs MEANS
  CONSIDER xs
    WHEN EMPTY              THEN 0
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY rest THEN x PLUS `running total` rest

@export
GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `all positive` IF
  CONSIDER xs
    WHEN EMPTY              THEN TRUE
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY rest THEN x GREATER THAN 0 AND `all positive` rest

@export
GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`first or zero` xs MEANS
  CONSIDER xs
    WHEN EMPTY              THEN 0
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY rest THEN x

-- `total from` is the case the positional decrease check gets wrong when it
-- confuses two coordinate systems. `srPos` is a position in the lowered HEAD,
-- which for a lambda-lifted WHERE helper begins with the enclosing decision's
-- parameters (`xs` and `floor` are threaded in, so `go`'s head is
-- `go(Xs, Floor, Ys, Out)` and the scrutinised argument is at 2) — while the
-- source call `go rest` writes only the helper's own argument, at 0. Reading the
-- head position out of the source arguments therefore fell off the end of the
-- list and reported this perfectly decreasing call as undecreasing. Every
-- recursive list helper written inside a parameterised decision hits it, and no
-- fixture did, because the two top-level recursions above have no enclosing
-- parameters and the offset is zero for them.
@export
GIVEN
  xs    IS A LIST OF NUMBER
  floor IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`total from` xs floor MEANS
  go xs
  WHERE
    GIVEN ys IS A LIST OF NUMBER
    GIVETH A NUMBER
    go ys MEANS
      CONSIDER ys
        WHEN EMPTY              THEN floor
        WHEN y FOLLOWED BY rest THEN y PLUS go rest

#EVAL `running total` (LIST 1, 2, 3)
-- L4 oracle ==> 6

#EVAL `running total` EMPTY
-- L4 oracle ==> 0

#EVAL `all positive` (LIST 1, 2, 3)
-- L4 oracle ==> TRUE

#EVAL `all positive` (LIST 1, 0, 3)
-- L4 oracle ==> FALSE

#EVAL `first or zero` (LIST 7, 8)
-- L4 oracle ==> 7

#EVAL `total from` (LIST 1, 2, 3) 10
-- L4 oracle ==> 16
